72 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 48 0 R
/T (When an AI programme stalls, four explanations are consistently reached for.)
/E (When an AI programme stalls, four explanations are consistently reached for.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
73 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 48 0 R
/T (The first is the technology argument. The model was not fit for purpose. The integration was too complex. The vendor overpromised. This has the advantage of being fixable. Replace the technology, run the programme again. Yet organisations that have replaced their AI infrastructure entirely frequently report exactly the same stall point. The technology changed. The outcome did not. Something else was the constraint.)
/E (The first is the technology argument. The model was not fit for purpose. The integration was too complex. The vendor overpromised. This has the advantage of being fixable. Replace the technology, run the programme again. Yet organisations that have replaced their AI infrastructure entirely frequently report exactly the same stall point. The technology changed. The outcome did not. Something else was the constraint.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
74 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 48 0 R
/T (The second is the data argument. The data is too fragmented, too inconsistent, too poorly governed for AI to work effectively. This is the most technically credible explanation and it is often partially true. But it raises a harder question: why, after years of investment in data infrastructure, do organisations still find themselves unable to make their data work for AI? Data quality failures are rarely a technical problem. They are an organisational one, the product of unclear ownership, absent strategic logic, and the accumulated consequence of decisions made without a coherent sense of what the data was ultimately for.)
/E (The second is the data argument. The data is too fragmented, too inconsistent, too poorly governed for AI to work effectively. This is the most technically credible explanation and it is often partially true. But it raises a harder question: why, after years of investment in data infrastructure, do organisations still find themselves unable to make their data work for AI? Data quality failures are rarely a technical problem. They are an organisational one, the product of unclear ownership, absent strategic logic, and the accumulated consequence of decisions made without a coherent sense of what the data was ultimately for.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
75 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 48 0 R
/T (The third is the skills argument. People do not know how to use these tools. Significant budget has flowed here, into reskilling programmes, AI literacy initiatives, and structured upskilling. Organisations that have run well-designed programmes at scale report that adoption remains shallow, that tools are used tentatively, and that behaviour reverts once structured support is removed. Skills were transferred. Something else prevented them from taking root.)
/E (The third is the skills argument. People do not know how to use these tools. Significant budget has flowed here, into reskilling programmes, AI literacy initiatives, and structured upskilling. Organisations that have run well-designed programmes at scale report that adoption remains shallow, that tools are used tentatively, and that behaviour reverts once structured support is removed. Skills were transferred. Something else prevented them from taking root.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
76 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 48 0 R
/T (The fourth is the change management argument. The organisation was not brought along. Communication was poor. Employees were not involved early enough. This is broad enough to absorb almost any failure, which is precisely what makes it dangerous. Change management as typically practised addresses how change is communicated, not whether the organisational conditions for that change genuinely exist.)
/E (The fourth is the change management argument. The organisation was not brought along. Communication was poor. Employees were not involved early enough. This is broad enough to absorb almost any failure, which is precisely what makes it dangerous. Change management as typically practised addresses how change is communicated, not whether the organisational conditions for that change genuinely exist.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
