/T (The first form is strategic incoherence. An organisation has invested significantly in AI. The technology is capable. The pilots are generating results. Eighteen months in, almost nothing has been scaled to enterprise level. The answer, on examination, is not in the technology. It is in the absence of a coherent strategic logic for what AI is actually for. Different functions are pursuing different definitions of success. Pilots proliferate because no one owns the question of what scaling requires. The organisation is active and busy, but it is not pointed anywhere. This pattern is one of the most common in AI transformation, and it is almost entirely invisible to standard diagnostic approaches.)
/E (The first form is strategic incoherence. An organisation has invested significantly in AI. The technology is capable. The pilots are generating results. Eighteen months in, almost nothing has been scaled to enterprise level. The answer, on examination, is not in the technology. It is in the absence of a coherent strategic logic for what AI is actually for. Different functions are pursuing different definitions of success. Pilots proliferate because no one owns the question of what scaling requires. The organisation is active and busy, but it is not pointed anywhere. This pattern is one of the most common in AI transformation, and it is almost entirely invisible to standard diagnostic approaches.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
96 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 50 0 R
/T (The second form is structural disconnection. Strong executive sponsorship. Visible commitment from the top. Genuine enthusiasm in the teams closest to the work. The programme stalls in the middle. Not through resistance, but because the layer of management responsible for translating strategic intent into operational behaviour was never given the permission, the capability, or the incentive to carry AI into their day-to-day decisions. The strategy exists. It never moves through the organisation.)
/E (The second form is structural disconnection. Strong executive sponsorship. Visible commitment from the top. Genuine enthusiasm in the teams closest to the work. The programme stalls in the middle. Not through resistance, but because the layer of management responsible for translating strategic intent into operational behaviour was never given the permission, the capability, or the incentive to carry AI into their day-to-day decisions. The strategy exists. It never moves through the organisation.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
97 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 50 0 R
/T (The third form is cultural contradiction. The workforce is willing. Curiosity about AI is present, and the resistance most leaders anticipate is largely absent. The tools are available. Training has been completed. Adoption remains shallow because the reward structures and performance frameworks point in a different direction from the stated  strategy. People understand what they are supposed to do. They can see that doing it)
/E (The third form is cultural contradiction. The workforce is willing. Curiosity about AI is present, and the resistance most leaders anticipate is largely absent. The tools are available. Training has been completed. Adoption remains shallow because the reward structures and performance frameworks point in a different direction from the stated  strategy. People understand what they are supposed to do. They can see that doing it)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
