241 0 obj
<<
/Title (Three sectors. The same root cause. These are not edge cases. They are the most consistent failure modes observed across industries, geographies and investment levels. And standard AI assessments, including technology audits, skills reviews and engagement surveys, would return a broadly positive read on all three.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 186 0 R
/Prev 298 0 R
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Title (The prior question, the one that should precede every AI investment decision, every board discussion on digital maturity, every vendor conversation, is this: does this organisation have the human layer capable of making AI work?)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 131 0 R
/Prev 187 0 R
/Next 299 0 R
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Title (These are not soft questions. They are the most operationally significant questions an organisation can ask before committing to a transformation programme. They are almost never asked systematically, measured rigorously, or used to sequence investment decisions.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 131 0 R
/Prev 300 0 R
/Next 188 0 R
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Title (That is not a technology problem. It is not a budget problem. It is a clarity problem. Organisations that understand where their human layer is limiting return, and address it with the same rigour they apply to their technology decisions, will compound their AI advantage through the rest of this decade. Organisations that keep reaching for the nearest familiar explanation will keep arriving at the same stall point.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 132 0 R
/Prev 189 0 R
/Next 301 0 R
>>
endobj
245 0 obj
<<
/Title (I have been working on what that requires. If the patterns in this piece have a name you have been unable to give them until now, I would find that conversation worth having.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 132 0 R
/Prev 301 0 R
/Next 190 0 R
>>
endobj
